<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="AppTiling" type="win32"/>

  <!--
    Windows 10 only, deliberately.

    Windows 10 and 11 share one GUID - Microsoft never issued a separate one for
    11 - so this single entry covers both and there is nothing to add for 11.
    What it excludes is Windows 8 and 8.1, which are not targets.

    Declaring the OS at all matters: an application that declares none is treated
    as designed for Windows 8, and several APIs then return compatibility-shimmed
    values rather than real ones.
  -->
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <!--
    Per-monitor-v2 DPI awareness, declared here rather than by calling
    SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext in code.

    A manifest applies before the process starts, so the awareness is settled
    before any coordinate is read. It also avoids a real hazard the code-based
    version had: calling SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext when the context is
    already set fails with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED, so keeping both would log a
    spurious warning on every run.

    It matters because App Tiling positions windows by SetWindowPos against
    monitor rectangles. A DPI-unaware process is handed virtualised coordinates
    for one and real ones for the other, and the windows land in the wrong
    place on any display above 100% scaling.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>

  <!--
    requireAdministrator - what makes double-clicking AppTiling.exe work at all.

    App Tiling arranges Task Manager and Open Hardware Monitor. Both commonly
    run elevated, and Windows refuses SetWindowPos from a lower-integrity
    process to a higher-integrity window: an unelevated run logs
    `Elevated: False`, fails both placements with Win32 error 5, and moves
    nothing.

    Without this, the default `asInvoker` applies and a double-clicked launch
    can never do its job - it fails silently with exit code 1 and no window to
    say so. A Task Scheduler entry registered with /RL HIGHEST elevates
    independently of the manifest either way.

    With this, double-click prompts for UAC and then works. An already-elevated
    scheduled-task launch is unaffected and sees no prompt.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="requireAdministrator" uiAccess="false"/>
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

</assembly>
